# Product

The public interface language is English. Commit messages are English. Do not rename host names.

- `ledgesec.com` and `www` are the site and the blog. The journal and visitor addresses are not there.
- `dash.ledgesec.com` is the site user's cabinet. `admin.ledgesec.com` is the admin cabinet. Both A records point at the European address. Do not add the resource domain in Coolify. `/api/` is open on both.
- The cabinet has no plans: a user has only the journal of their site, without addresses. An admin does not open the journal on the client host, and a user does not open it on the admin host.
- An admin is a row in `users`. The API does not create that row on startup and does not overwrite it from env.
- An admin sees addresses on every edge. A user sees the requests of their hosts, without addresses. Paths such as `/.env`, `/.htaccess`, `/.git`, `wp-login`, dumps, and database panels are highlighted as a probe. An admin adds a template in Markers under Probes. The mark is computed when the journal is read. `/.env` inside a directory is a probe too.
- On dash a user adds their host and chooses how it reports observations. They can delete their own host. An admin sees everyone's hosts and can delete them too. When an admin adds a host, they choose the user who owns it. That user sees the lines for `/etc/ledgesec/agent.env` in their own cabinet, together with the ingest secret. Deleting a host does not erase the journal: requests already written stay. The choices that work now are nginx, the Linux daemon, and the Coolify collector. DNS and Apache are listed and are not available yet. A host is the whole web server. Every request on it goes into the journal, including a name or path the server does not have: that is how a scan shows up. The daemon reads the nginx log and does not change the request. The Coolify collector reads the whole proxy log and does not change the request. `LEDGESEC_SITE_ID` is the host identifier. A site is the Host header name the proxy passed to the application: the row is created when such a batch is accepted. A name that only hit the catch-all stays in the journal and does not become a site. A disabled site is not written further. After install, the wizard waits for that site and closes when the name arrives.
- A journal row lives for 3 years, then ClickHouse drops it. Deleting a host does not shorten that term.
- The cabinet shows what is in the journal: requests, probes, edges, sites, hours, the country code, and addresses for an admin. Probes, requests, and sites are separate live pages. A probe path and a slow path open the requests for that path. A site shows its requests and probes for the day, and RPS for the minute. The edge on the sites page is visible to an admin. A request row shows the time, method, status, and User-Agent when the collector sent them. The country is taken at ingest from a local GeoIP database, with no request to the outside. On Overview an admin sees a country map from the journal code, total RPS, and the RPS of each domain for the last minute. The latest 30 requests are there too. A new row flashes and fades. The request, probe, site, and address tables have search and a filter on the row fields. Search and filter live in the page address. Search does not fire on every character, only after a pause. While it runs, the table is dimmed and stays on screen. Addresses are a separate page. An address has one Actions button: details, the requests for that address, and copy. Block and challenge are visible in the menu and disabled. Do not draw CVE, node CPU, incidents, MTTD/MTTR, compliance reports, an analyst seat, or a blocked-threat counter. Observation does not block.
- The first customers are the Lua module in observation mode. The request reaches their application. A row has no body, cookie, query, or Referer. Method, status, User-Agent, response size, protocol, and TLS are written when the collector sent them. Challenge and deny are not turned on.
- Europe stores Postgres, ClickHouse, the API, and the site. New York is only the edge from `docker-compose.us.yml`. Do not copy the journal or the cabinet to New York.
- The two A records are a spare path, not geo and not anycast. There is no authoritative DNS of our own. We do not take over the customer's zone.
- Caddy on the edge listens on 80 and 443. The Coolify proxy is Custom (None). The compose deployment is Raw. Do not set `CADDY_LOCAL_CERTS` on the servers.
- Secrets live only in env. They are not in git. Do not commit `.env`.
